Subject: winemac.drv: do not let applications set the display gamma by default

A DirectDraw test blacked the entire Mac display on exit, and it stayed
black after the process was gone: wined3d writes the saved gamma ramp back
when a swapchain is torn down, and it had never checked that the read which
produced that ramp succeeded, so it wrote zeroes. Nothing undoes that. The
ramp is display state, owned by no process, so the desktop stays dark until
something else sets one -- and the user cannot see well enough to do it.

The wined3d bug is worth fixing on its own (patch 0024), but it is not the
only way to reach a bad ramp, and the display is the one resource a user
cannot recover from once it is gone. On a platform where a prefix is a
sandbox for one application, letting that application reach out and change
the whole machine's display is not a trade worth making by default.

CrossOver reaches the same conclusion from the other direction: its stock
bottle ships "AllowSetGamma"=dword:00000000, with the wined3d code left
exactly as upstream has it. This tree targets one platform, so the default
belongs in the driver rather than in every prefix.

The registry key still works, so a prefix that genuinely needs gamma -- an
old game that dims the screen for a fade -- can set it back to 1.
diff --git a/dlls/winemac.drv/macdrv_main.c b/dlls/winemac.drv/macdrv_main.c
index ed91383..770015b 100644
--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/macdrv_main.c
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/macdrv_main.c
@@ -45,7 +45,16 @@ C_ASSERT(NUM_EVENT_TYPES <= sizeof(macdrv_event_mask) * 8);
 int topmost_float_inactive = TOPMOST_FLOAT_INACTIVE_NONFULLSCREEN;
 bool capture_displays_for_fullscreen = false;
 BOOL allow_vsync = TRUE;
-BOOL allow_set_gamma = TRUE;
+/* Default off on macOS. The gamma ramp is display state that no process owns:
+ * an application that sets one and then exits leaves the desktop under it, and
+ * a ramp of zeroes leaves the whole screen black with no way to recover from
+ * the machine itself. wined3d reaches this on every swapchain teardown, and it
+ * restores a ramp it never checked it had read -- so a failed read blacks the
+ * display of a user who never asked any application to touch gamma.
+ * HKCU\Software\Wine\Mac Driver "AllowSetGamma"=1 turns it back on for a
+ * prefix that wants it. CrossOver ships the same conclusion as a per-bottle
+ * setting; this tree targets one platform, so it is the default. */
+BOOL allow_set_gamma = FALSE;
 bool left_option_is_alt = false;
 bool right_option_is_alt = false;
 bool left_command_is_ctrl = false;
